{"records":[{"id":"yw5gg8sx22","title":"Integrated multi-omics analyses identify anti-viral host factors and pathways controlling SARS-CoV-2 infection","description":"RNAseq profiling of A549 cells following knockout of VTA1, DAZAP2, and KLF4, as well as non-targeting control (NC) gRNA.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-09T20:36:44.348Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yw5gg8sx22","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yw5gg8sx22"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"McGrail","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pjr7b8sbf8","title":"\"Impaired local intrinsic immunity to SARS-CoV-2 infection in severe COVID-19\". Ziegler et al.","description":"SARS-CoV-2 infection can cause severe respiratory COVID-19. However, many individuals present with isolated upper respiratory symptoms, suggesting potential to constrain viral pathology to the nasopharynx. Which cells are the primary targets of SARS-CoV-2 and how infection influences the respiratory epithelium remains incompletely understood. We performed scRNA-seq on nasopharyngeal swabs from 58 healthy and COVID-19 participants. During COVID-19, we observe expansion of secretory, loss of ciliated, and epithelial cell repopulation via deuterosomal expansion. In mild/moderate COVID-19, epithelial cells express anti-viral/interferon-responsive genes, while cells in severe COVID-19 have muted anti-viral responses despite equivalent viral loads. SARS-CoV-2 RNA+ host-target cells are highly heterogenous, including developing ciliated, interferon-responsive ciliated, AZGP1high goblet, and KRT13+ “hillock”-like cells, and we identify genes likely involved in susceptibility, resistance, or response to infection. We define protective and detrimental responses to SARS-CoV-2, the direct viral targets of infection, and suggest that failed epithelial anti-viral immunity may underlie severe COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-23T16:12:52.927Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pjr7b8sbf8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pjr7b8sbf8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Carly","last_name":"Ziegler","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Vincent","last_name":"Miao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anna","last_name":"Owings","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Navia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ying","last_name":"Tang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Bromley","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peter","last_name":"Lotfy","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Meredith","last_name":"Sloan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hannah","last_name":"Laird","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haley","last_name":"Williams","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Micayla","last_name":"George","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Riley","last_name":"Drake","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Taylor","last_name":"Christian","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adam","last_name":"Parker","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Campbell","last_name":"Sindel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Molly","last_name":"Burger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yilianys","last_name":"Pride","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mohammad","last_name":"Hasan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Abraham","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michal","last_name":"Senitko","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tanya","last_name":"Robinson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alex","last_name":"Shalek","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sarah","last_name":"Glover","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bruce","last_name":"Horwitz","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jose","last_name":"Ordovas-Montanes","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Systems Biology","Transcriptomics","Respiratory Epithelium","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"743ty4chyz","title":"Functional Landscape of SARS-CoV-2 Cellular Restriction, Martin-Sancho et al.","description":"A deficient interferon response to SARS-CoV-2 infection has been implicated as a determinant of severe COVID-19. To identify the molecular effectors that govern interferon control of SARS-CoV-2 infection, we conducted a large-scale gain-of-function analysis that evaluated the impact of human interferon stimulated genes (ISGs) on viral replication. A limited subset of ISGs were found to control viral infection, including endosomal factors inhibiting viral entry, RNA binding proteins suppressing viral RNA synthesis, and a highly enriched cluster of ER-Golgi-resident ISGs inhibiting viral assembly-egress. These included broad-acting antiviral ISGs, and eight ISGs that specifically inhibited SARS-CoV-2 and -1 replication. Amongst the broad-acting ISGs was BST2/tetherin, which impeded viral release, and is antagonized by SARS-CoV-2 Orf7a protein. Overall, these data illuminate a set of ISGs that underlie innate immune control of SARS-CoV-2/-1 infection, which will facilitate the understanding of host determinants that impact disease severity and offer potential therapeutic strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-04-01T22:45:14.73Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/743ty4chyz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/743ty4chyz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Martin-Sancho","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virology","Interferon","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"57j37g6kdh","title":"High-resolution photocatalytic mapping of SARS-CoV-2 spike interactions on the cell surface. Datta et al","description":"Identifying virus-host interactions on the cell surface can improve our understanding of viral entry and pathogenesis. SARS-CoV-2, the causative agent of the COVID-19 disease, uses ACE2 as a receptor to enter cells. Yet the full repertoire of cell surface proteins that contribute to viral entry is unknown. We developed a photocatalyst-based viral-host protein microenvironment mapping platform (ViraMap) to probe the molecular neighborhood of the SARS-CoV-2 spike protein on the human cell surface. Application of ViraMap to ACE2-expressing cells captured ACE2, the established co-receptor NRP1, and several novel cell surface proteins. We systematically analyzed the relevance of these candidate proteins to SARS-CoV-2 entry by knockdown and overexpression approaches in pseudovirus and authentic infection models and identified PTGFRN and EFNB1 as bona fide viral entry factors. Our results highlight additional host targets that participate in SARS-CoV-2 infection and showcase ViraMap as a powerful platform for defining viral interactions on the cell surface.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-07T06:19:10.123Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/57j37g6kdh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/57j37g6kdh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mohsan","last_name":"Saeed","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Suprama","last_name":"Datta","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gcdpsxd6d5","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:41:07.557Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gcdpsxd6d5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gcdpsxd6d5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","RNA Knockdown"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"72tr8jj8y4","title":"RNAi screening using HK-2 cells: insights into the roles of soluble ACE2 in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Yeung et al","description":"Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) can cause acute respiratory disease and multiorgan failure. Finding human host factors that are essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection could facilitate the formulation of treatment strategies. Using a human kidney cell line—HK-2—that is highly susceptible to SARS-CoV-2, we performed a genome-wide RNAi screening and identified virus dependency factors (VDFs), which play regulatory roles in biological pathways linked to clinical manifestations of SARS-CoV-2 infection. Importantly, we discovered an overlooked role of a secretory form of SARS-CoV-2 receptor, soluble angiotensin converting enzyme 2 (sACE2), in SARS-CoV-2 infection. Further investigations revealed that SARS-CoV-2 exploits receptor-mediated endocytosis through interaction between its spike with sACE2 or sACE2-vasopressin via AT1 or AVPR1B, respectively. Our identification of new VDFs and the regulatory effect of sACE2 on SARS-CoV-2 infection shed new insights into pathogenesis and cell entry mechanism of SARS-CoV-2 as well as potential treatment strategies for COVID-19.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T23:48:03.555Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/72tr8jj8y4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/72tr8jj8y4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Man Lung","last_name":"Yeung","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Vasopressin","Screening","RNA Knockdown","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7bd5bhmhmz","title":"Genome-scale identification of SARS-CoV-2 and pan-coronavirus host factor networks. Schneider et al.","description":"The COVID-19 pandemic has claimed the lives of more than one million people worldwide. The causative agent, SARS-CoV-2, is a member of the Coronaviridae family, which are viruses that cause respiratory infections of varying severity. The cellular host factors and pathways co-opted by SARS- CoV-2 and other coronaviruses in the execution of their life cycles remain ill-defined. To develop an extensive compendium of host factors required for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and three seasonal coronaviruses (HCoV-OC43, HCoV-NL63, and HCoV-229E), we performed parallel genome-scale CRISPR knockout screens. These screens uncovered multiple host factors and pathways with pan- coronavirus and virus-specific functional roles, including major dependency on glycosaminoglycan biosynthesis, SREBP signaling, BMP signaling, and glycosylphosphatidylinositol biosynthesis, as well as a requirement for several poorly characterized proteins. We identified an absolute requirement for the VTT-domain containing protein TMEM41B for infection by SARS-CoV-2 and three seasonal coronaviruses. This human Coronaviridae host factor compendium represents a rich resource to develop new therapeutic strategies for acute COVID-19 and potential future coronavirus spillover events.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-12-03T09:15:53.589Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7bd5bhmhmz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7bd5bhmhmz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"JT","last_name":"Poirier","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4jd92vrymf","title":"Excel Data S1 - Differentially Expressed Metabolites","description":"The files capture the relationship between viral load and microbial metabolic activity across four main comparison groups: non-detected virus (ND) versus high viral load, ND versus medium viral load, medium versus low viral load, and medium versus high viral load. This comprehensive dataset offers detailed insights into how SARS-CoV-2 infection affects nasopharyngeal microbiome metabolic activities, specifically highlighting viral load-dependent changes in key pathways, such as amino sugar metabolism, fatty acid degradation, and nucleotide sugar biosynthesis. These findings represent a valuable resource for understanding host-microbe interactions during COVID-19 infection and provide insights into the biochemical mechanisms that may contribute to disease pathogenesis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-24T07:53:36.876Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4jd92vrymf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4jd92vrymf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zi-Lun","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yang-Di","last_name":"Su ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yi-Yao","last_name":"Hsu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hsiu-Hsien","last_name":"Lin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuan-Yu","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yi-An","last_name":"Tai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hung-Chieh","last_name":"Su","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mao-Wang","last_name":"Ho","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Po-Ren","last_name":"Hsueh","orcid":"0000-0002-7502-9225"},{"first_name":"Der-Yang","last_name":"Cho","orcid":"0000-0002-6429-3565"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Differential Metabolite"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wk4n2fvmp7","title":"Conserved Antagonism of cGAS-STING Signaling by NSP2 Proteins from SARS-CoV-2 and SARSr-CoV-2","description":"Despite the high risk of host-shift associated with SARSr-CoV-2, the extent to which these viruses share similar or distinct molecular mechanisms in antagonizing host innate immune responses, compared to SARS-CoV-2, remains largely unexplored. RIG-Ⅰ-like receptors (RLRs) and cGAS-STING signaling pathways function as critical innate immune sensors that detect viral infection and trigger IFN-Ⅰ production to restrict viral replication.Whether SARS-CoV-2 modulates the cGAS-STING pathway remains incompletely understood. Here, we demonstrate that SARSr-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV-2 NSP2 target STING to suppress type Ⅰ IFN production. Mechanistically, SARSr-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV-2 NSP2 interact with STING and impair STING oligomerization and aggregation. Furthermore, NSP2 attenuates the association between STING and TBK1 and inhibits STING translocation from the endoplasmic reticulum to the Golgi apparatus. As a consequence, NSP2 suppresses cGAS-STING-mediated phosphorylation of TBK1 and IRF3, impairs IRF3 nuclear translocation, and ultimately reduces type Ⅰ IFN production. Taken together, these findings suggest that SARSr-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV-2 NSP2 impair the cGAS-STING signaling pathway and provide insights for developing antiviral strategies against coronavirus.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-28T12:54:10.699Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wk4n2fvmp7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wk4n2fvmp7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xuan","last_name":"Lv","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaoyuan","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Riwei","last_name":"Huang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lei","last_name":"Cheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jiang","last_name":"Rao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Fangting","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xingchen","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xuanjue","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yongyi","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"U24A20363"},{"id":null,"name":"Guangdong Major Project of Basic and Applied Basic Research","grant_id":"2020B0301030007"},{"id":null,"name":"Guangdong Provincial Key R&D Program","grant_id":"2022B1111040001 "}],"categories":["Innate Immune Response","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rcxddvxv3c","title":"Impact of vaccination on intra-host genetic diversity of patients infected with SARS-CoV-2 Gamma lineage","description":"The high transmissibility, rapid evolution, and immune escape of SARS-CoV-2 variants can influence the course of infection and, in turn, morbidity and mortality in COVID-19, posing a challenge in controlling transmission rates and contributing to the emergence and spread of new variants. The factors that shape viral genetic variation are consequently essential for understanding the evolution and transmission of SARS-CoV-2, especially in vaccinated individuals where immune response plays a role in the progression and spread of this disease. This study assessed. In this context, we evaluated the impact of immunity induced by the CoronaVac vaccine (Butantan/Sinovac) on intra-host genetic diversity, analyzing 118 whole-genome sequences of SARS-CoV-2 from unvaccinated and vaccinated patients infected with the Gamma variant. Vaccination with CoronaVac favors negative selection at the intra-host level in different genomic regions and prevent greater genetic diversity of SARS-CoV-2, which may help reduce the emergence of new mutations, reinforcing the importance of vaccination in reducing virus transmission.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-05T08:45:15.632Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rcxddvxv3c","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rcxddvxv3c"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Beatriz de Carvalho","last_name":"Marques","orcid":"0000-0003-1512-0198"},{"first_name":"Cecília","last_name":"Banho","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Livia","last_name":"Sacchetto","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nikos","last_name":"Vasilakis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mauricio","last_name":"Nogueira","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Vaccination","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":122619,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":138,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":122619,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":122038,"restricted":63,"embargoed":518},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2113},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":645},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":451},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":401},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":400},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":388},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":378},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":308},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":235},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":230}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":120216},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1709},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":176},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":125},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":73},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":50},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":37},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":26}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":464},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":405},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":351},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":314},{"id":"48c8fa0f-965c-5cbf-a859-a5363432478b","name":"Universidade de Sao Paulo","count":275}]}}